Interview Answer: Explain Your Project

I worked on a Telekom broadband data-engineering project. The main objective was to build reliable and incremental data pipelines for broadband-related domains such as Customer, Broadband Product, Product Pricing, Subscription, Billing, Daily Usage, Network Performance, and Service Fault data.

The project followed a medallion architecture with Landing, Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers. Azure Data Factory was used for ingestion, scheduling, orchestration, parameterization, auditing, and failure handling. Azure Databricks was used for distributed transformations and data-quality processing. ADLS Gen2 was used as the data lake, and Azure Synapse Dedicated SQL Pool was used as the final data warehouse for reporting.

One of the pipelines I developed was the Broadband Product pipeline. The source was a SQL Server database hosted inside the Telekom data centre. Since the database was outside the Azure network, ADF connected to it through a Self-hosted Integration Runtime. The SQL Server linked service used a dedicated read-only service account. The password was securely stored in Azure Key Vault and referenced from the linked service.

The pipeline ran daily using an ADF Schedule Trigger. At the start of every run, ADF generated a unique Pipeline Run ID and Batch ID. The Batch ID was created using the entity name, scheduled business date, and part of the ADF Run ID. We used the same Batch ID across Landing, Bronze, Silver, Gold, Synapse, and audit tables for complete traceability.

For incremental loading, I used two Lookup activities. The first Lookup retrieved the previous successful watermark from a control table. The second Lookup retrieved the current maximum modified_timestamp from the Broadband Product source table.

The Copy Activity extracted records using the condition:

