The extracted records were written as Snappy-compressed Parquet files into an ADLS Gen2 Landing folder organized by business date and Batch ID. This ensured that each batch had its own immutable folder and could be independently traced or reprocessed.

After extraction, I validated that the number of rows read from SQL Server matched the number of rows copied into Landing. ADF then passed the exact Landing batch path, Batch ID, Pipeline Run ID, business date, watermark range, and table names to a Databricks notebook using Notebook Activity Base parameters.

The Landing-to-Bronze notebook received these parameters through Databricks widgets and read only the current Batch ID folder. It added technical audit columns such as Batch ID, Pipeline Run ID, business date, watermark values, Landing path, and ingestion timestamp. The records were appended into a managed Bronze Delta table. Bronze preserved the source data and incremental history.

The Bronze-to-Silver notebook filtered the Bronze table using the current Batch ID. It trimmed spaces, converted blanks to null, standardized Product IDs, Product Family, Product Status, and Product Name, and converted fields into appropriate business datatypes.

For example, speed values such as 500 Mbps and 1 Gbps were standardized into a common product_speed_mbps column. 1 Gbps became 1000 Mbps. Download limits were converted into a numeric download_limit_gb column with a separate is_unlimited_download Boolean flag. Contract values such as 12 months were converted into integer months. Launch Date, End Date, and Modified Timestamp were safely converted into Date and Timestamp datatypes.

After completing the transformations, I created Boolean validation flags for critical business rules. These included Product ID format validation, mandatory Product Name validation, approved Product Family and Product Status checks, positive Product Speed validation, valid Contract Months, valid dates, and valid Modified Timestamp.

Blocking failures were added to a rejection-reasons array. Optional issues were added to a warning-reasons array. Based on these results, every record received one of three statuses:

