Accepted and deduplicated records were loaded into the Silver Delta table using Delta MERGE. New Product IDs were inserted. Existing Product IDs were updated only when the incoming Modified Timestamp was newer. Older or equal records were ignored, which made the Silver pipeline incremental and idempotent.

In the Gold layer, I created a warehouse-ready Broadband Product dimension. For Synapse loading, Databricks prepared a DataFrame containing only the applicable records from the current Batch ID and wrote it as Parquet into a dynamic ADLS export path containing the business date and Batch ID.

ADF used the same export-path parameter in the next Copy Activity, so it loaded only the current batch’s Parquet files into the Synapse staging table. Before loading the final dimension, we performed row-count reconciliation, null Product ID checks, duplicate Product ID checks, Product Status validation, positive-speed validation, and Contract Month validation.

After all validations passed, ADF executed a Synapse stored procedure. The procedure updated existing Product records when the incoming Modified Timestamp was newer and inserted Product IDs that did not already exist in DimBroadbandProduct.

Finally, the pipeline updated the audit record as successful and advanced the source watermark only after the entire pipeline, including the Synapse target load, completed successfully.

We also designed stage-level recovery. If Landing-to-Bronze failed, we reused the existing Landing batch. If Silver failed, we reprocessed the existing Bronze Batch ID. If only Synapse failed, we reused the existing Gold Parquet export and restarted from Synapse staging instead of extracting the SQL Server records again.

This design provided incremental processing, batch-level traceability, data-quality enforcement, duplicate prevention, reconciliation, idempotency, and restartability.

Short One-Minute Version

I worked on an end-to-end Telekom broadband data-engineering project using ADF, ADLS Gen2, Azure Databricks, Delta Lake, Azure Key Vault, and Azure Synapse.

For the Broadband Product pipeline, the source was SQL Server inside the Telekom data centre. ADF connected through Self-hosted Integration Runtime and used a dedicated service account whose password was secured in Key Vault.

The pipeline ran daily and used two Lookup activities for watermark-based incremental extraction. The first retrieved the previous successful watermark, and the second retrieved the current maximum Modified Timestamp. The Copy Activity extracted records between those values and wrote them as Parquet into an ADLS folder partitioned by business date and Batch ID.

ADF passed the path and Batch ID to Databricks through Notebook Base parameters. Databricks loaded the current batch into Bronze, standardized and validated it in Silver, quarantined invalid records, deduplicated Product IDs, and used Delta MERGE for inserts and updates.

Gold created a warehouse-ready Product dimension and exported the current batch as Parquet. ADF loaded it into Synapse staging, validated row counts, null keys, duplicates, and business rules, and then called a stored procedure to update and insert records into the final dimension.

The watermark was updated only after complete success. Because every layer was persisted and tracked using the Batch ID, we could restart from the failed stage without repeating successful upstream processing.

